By symmetry $y\mapsto G_\Omega(x,y)$ has the required regularity. Apply the published second Green identity to it and $u$ on $\Omega\setminus\overline B_\varepsilon(x)$. The outer term with $G\partial_
u u$ vanishes and the remaining outer term is $-u\partial_{
u_y}G=P_\Omega u$. On the inner sphere the $G\partial_
u u$ term tends to zero at rates $O(\varepsilon)$ or $O(\varepsilon|\log\varepsilon|)$; the singular term $-u\partial_{
u_{ann}}G$ tends to $-u(x)$ because the annulus inner normal points toward the pole. Thus $\int G\Delta u=\int_{\partial\Omega}P_\Omega u-u(x)$, which rearranges to the displayed formula. Polar integrability permits passage through the volume term. Since $G>0$ inside and has zero trace, its outward derivative is nonpositive wherever it exists, so $P\ge0$. Substitute $u\equiv1$ to get boundary mass one.
